== Geography ==
The Kebnekaise massif lies within a range of the Scandinavian mountains that is bordered by the glacial valleys Ladtjovagge (Sami spelling Láddjuvággi), Tjäktjavagge (Čeakčavággi) and Vistasvagge (Visttasvággi). The highest points of the massif lie along the ridge (called "Vargryggen" in Swedish) that runs from the southern and northern summits to Kebnepakte (Giebmebákti) at 1,981 metres (6,499 ft). Other subsidiary peaks are Kebnetjåkka (Giebmečohkka), Vierranvárri, Tolpagorni 1,662 metres (5,453 ft) , Guobircohkka 1,506 metres (4,941 ft), and Siŋŋibákti...","coordinates":[{"lat":67.90444444,"lon":18.52833333,"primary":true,"globe":"earth"}],"pageimage":"Kebnekaise_viewed_from_Tarfala_valley_-_narrower_crop.jpg","pageprops":{"page_image_free":"Kebnekaise_viewed_from_Tarfala_valley_-_narrower_crop.jpg","wikibase-shortdesc":"Sweden's highest mountain","wikibase_item":"Q214011"}}]}}
